course: dictionary
lesson: 11
title: The word ability
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Ability means the power or skill to do something. You might have the ability to swim, or the ability to ride a bike. «Free will is the ability to make your own choices» uses the word in exactly this way: it is about being able to choose.
explain: An ability can also be a special talent, something a person is good at. When someone says «to the best of your ability», they mean as well as you can. Read the words around the word to find which meaning fits.
explain: In science, the word describes what animals and plants can do, such as the ability to fly or change colour. In stories it can mean a superhero's powers. The clues in the sentence tell you which sense is being used.
fact: Ability is a noun: an ability, abilities.
fact: «Ability» means the power or skill to do something.
fact: «Ability» can also mean how well someone can do something, as in «reading ability».
fact: «Disability» is a different word, for a condition that makes some things harder to do.
fact: Modal verbs such as can, could and must help a main verb and show «certainty, ability, permission or duty».
exercise: «Free will is the ability to make your own choices.» What does ability mean here? | A kind of bird that cannot fly | *The power or skill to do something | A loud noise | A rule that stops you playing
exercise: Mia says, «She has great reading ability.» What does ability mean here? | *How well someone can read | The time it takes to finish a book | A library card | A book with lots of pictures
exercise: Which word is the noun for the power or skill to do something? | *ability | quickly | able | abled
test: Coach says, «Give it your best, to the best of your ability.» What does this mean? | *As well as you can | Without any help from anyone | Very quickly, with no care | Only if you feel like it
test: Which word names a condition that makes some things harder to do? | ticket | talent | ability | *disability
exam: In which sentence does the modal verb can show ability? | Tom swam across the lake yesterday | *Tom can swim across the lake | Tom is swimming across the lake now | Tom swims across the lake for fun
